--- !u!114 &700177444
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 700177441}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 20f0d826d695a47609677e1975e555e3, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  notes:
  - reference: {fileID: 700177441}
    note: 'Sample overview: This area demonstrates a basic touchable object setup
      combined with UnityEventLibrary''s event sequencing system. Explore the objects
      below to understand how they work together.'
  - reference: {fileID: 635229007}
    note: "TouchManager \u2014 Required component for every touchable group. It listens
      to touch input on a specific layer (here: \"Touchable2\") and broadcasts a
      universal OnTouch event for any object touched within the group.\n\nTo add
      a new touchable group: open the Layer dropdown at the top of this Inspector
      \u2192 \"Add Layer\" \u2192 name a new empty slot (e.g. \"Touchable3\"). Then
      create a new TouchManager and assign its \"Touchable Layer\" to that layer.\n\nNote:
      If your group only has one touchable object, assigning events on the manager
      or directly on the object is equivalent."
  - reference: {fileID: 232611671}
    note: "Touchable Object \u2014 This is the actual object that receives touch
      input. Its touch events are intentionally left empty because all event handling
      is delegated to the TouchManager above. \n\nYou need to make the layer of all
      touchable objects to the \"Touchable Layer\" as the one selected in the TouchManager.
      It doesn't need to be the children of the manager but their layers of the touchable
      objects matters."
  - reference: {fileID: 2095859089}
    note: "UnityEventLibrary Sequence: \"CountDownAndToast\" \u2014 An event sequence
      defined on this object. Copy the sequence name exactly when referencing it
      in code or other components.\n\nWhat it does: triggers the ToastManager to
      display a new toast every 1 second, cycling through different icons as a countdown.
      This demonstrates how to chain timed events using UnityEventLibrary."
  - reference: {fileID: 635229007}
    note: "Lock / Unlock pattern \u2014 To prevent duplicate triggers during a sequence,
      the TouchManager exposes Lock() and Unlock() methods.\n\nIn this example: the
      first step of CountDownAndToast calls TouchManager.Lock(), disabling further
      touch input. The final step calls TouchManager.Unlock(), re-enabling it. Use
      this pattern whenever a sequence must run to completion before accepting new
      input.\n\nNote1: Other TouchManager will not be locked if you don't call theirs!\n\nNote2:
      Toasts replace the previous toast immediately rather than stacking, keeping
      the UI clean during the sequence."
