--- !u!114 &894345193
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 894345190}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 20f0d826d695a47609677e1975e555e3, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  notes:
  - reference: {fileID: 894345190}
    note: Here is the example for Interaction Trigger, Move and Rotate.
  - reference: {fileID: 581218477}
    note: "1. This object triggers the move & rotate event. When you select it, a
      green wireframe appears, showing the boundaries of the trigger area.\n\n2.
      The trigger uses a Collider with \u201CIs Trigger\u201D enabled. You can adjust
      its center and size to control the active area.\n\n3. When the player enters
      the trigger area, \u201CFirstEnter\u201D (only the first time) and \u201CEnter\u201D
      (every time) are called. When the player leaves the area, \u201CExit\u201D
      is called (every time).\n\n4. On the first entry, it activates the typewriter
      effect to display and replace the text. At the same time\u2014and on every
      later entry\u2014the red text moves back and forth between its original position
      and the \u201CMoving Target,\u201D while also rotating.\n\n5. The \u201CNotice\u201D
      object uses a LazyFollow component. This allows an object to follow the camera
      and rotate toward it. In this case, following is disabled by setting it to
      \u201CNone.\u201D The rotation mode is set to \u201CLook At with World Up,\u201D
      which means it faces the camera but keeps its upright orientation (it won\u2019t
      tilt sideways)."
